      "initial_content": {
        "title": "Tomato Pasta: preparation and background",
        "source_event_sequences": [
          70,
          71,
          72
        ],
        "body": "Tomato pasta is pasta tossed with a cooked tomato sauce—typically ripe tomatoes or passata reduced with olive oil, salt and aromatic ingredients such as garlic, onion or basil. The pasta is boiled separately, then combined with the sauce so it finishes coating the noodles; cheese may be added, but the menu’s vegetarian label does not establish a particular recipe.\n\nIts background is distinctly transregional. Dry pasta developed through Mediterranean and Arab-world traditions, while tomatoes came to Europe from the Americas in the sixteenth century and were initially treated with suspicion. Tomato sauces appeared in Italian cooking by the seventeenth century, but tomato-on-pasta combinations became established later, especially in Naples and across the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Thus “tomato pasta” is best understood as a later Italian classic built from ingredients with different histories, not as an unchanged ancient dish.\n\nSources: [Treccani, “Pasta”](https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/pasta_%28Enciclopedia-dei-ragazzi%29/); [Museo del Pomodoro, “Il pomodoro nella storia”](https://pomodoro.museidelcibo.it/per-saperne-di-piu/focus-su/pomodoro-a-parma/pomodoro-nella-storia-tullio-gregory/); [La Cucina Italiana, “Spaghetti al pomodoro”](https://www.lacucinaitaliana.it/article/spaghetti-al-pomodoro-origini-del-piatto-simbolo-italiano/)",
        "summary": "Tomato pasta is a simple vegetarian pasta dish dressed with cooked tomato sauce; its modern Italian identity emerged gradually through the meeting of pasta, New World tomatoes and later Italian cooking practices."
      },
      "revised_content": {
        "title": "Tomato Pasta: preparation and background",
        "source_event_sequences": [
          70,
          71,
          72
        ],
        "body": "Tomato pasta is pasta with cooked tomato sauce—usually ripe tomatoes or passata, olive oil and salt, with optional garlic, onion or basil. Boil the pasta separately, then toss it with the sauce until coated. Cheese may be added, but the menu’s vegetarian label does not specify a particular recipe.\n\nIts background is distinctly transregional. Dry pasta developed through Mediterranean and Arab-world traditions, while tomatoes came to Europe from the Americas in the sixteenth century and were initially treated with suspicion. Tomato sauces appeared in Italian cooking by the seventeenth century, but tomato-on-pasta combinations became established later, especially in Naples and across the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Thus “tomato pasta” is best understood as a later Italian classic built from ingredients with different histories, not as an unchanged ancient dish.\n\nSources: [Treccani, “Pasta”](https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/pasta_%28Enciclopedia-dei-ragazzi%29/); [Museo del Pomodoro, “Il pomodoro nella storia”](https://pomodoro.museidelcibo.it/per-saperne-di-piu/focus-su/pomodoro-a-parma/pomodoro-nella-storia-tullio-gregory/); [La Cucina Italiana, “Spaghetti al pomodoro”](https://www.lacucinaitaliana.it/article/spaghetti-al-pomodoro-origini-del-piatto-simbolo-italiano/)",
        "summary": "Tomato pasta is a simple vegetarian pasta dish dressed with cooked tomato sauce; its modern Italian identity emerged gradually through the meeting of pasta, New World tomatoes and later Italian cooking practices."
      }
