        ],
        "body": "## Lentil soup\n\n**What it is.** Lentil soup is a savory soup built around cooked lentils, whose texture may range from brothy and chunky to smooth and puréed. Lentils have been staple foods in the Near East and India, and are widely used in soups, stews, salads, and other dishes.[[1]](https://repo.mel.cgiar.org/items/9df3d770-56ad-4214-b16a-f26d4052afb8)\n\n**Typical preparation.** A documented Egyptian-style method rinses red or yellow lentils, briefly boils and drains them, then cooks them with browned onion and carrot, tomato, and broth. The mixture is blended smooth, seasoned with salt and cumin, reheated, and served with toasted bread cubes and lime; garlic, parsley, or extra tomato are noted variations.[[2]](https://www.ucpress.edu/blog-posts/a-tomato-y-lentil-soup-for-cooler-days)\n\n**Background.** Lentils are among the earliest domesticated pulse crops, with archaeological remains in the Fertile Crescent dating back about 10,000 years. Evidence cited by ICARDA includes lentil paste in ancient Egyptian tombs and a depiction of lentil soup from the era of Ramses II. The menu does not identify a specific national style, so these details describe a well-attested tradition rather than claiming this exact bowl is Egyptian.[[1]](https://repo.mel.cgiar.org/items/9df3d770-56ad-4214-b16a-f26d4052afb8)",
        "summary": "Lentil soup is a flexible, protein-rich pulse soup; the menu names the dish but gives no regional variant."
      },
      "revised_content": {
        "title": "Lentil soup",
        "source_event_sequences": [
          67,
