        ],
        "body": "## Lentil soup\n\n**What it is.** Lentil soup is a savory soup built around cooked lentils, ranging from brothy and chunky to smooth and puréed. Lentils have long been staples in the Near East and India and are widely used in soups, stews, salads, and other dishes.[[1]](https://repo.mel.cgiar.org/items/9df3d770-56ad-4214-b16a-f26d4052afb8)\n\n**Typical preparation.** Rinse red or yellow lentils, boil them briefly with salt, and drain. Brown chopped onion and carrot in butter, then combine them with the lentils, tomato, and broth. Blend until smooth, reheat, and season with salt and cumin. Serve with toasted bread cubes and lime; garlic, parsley, or extra tomato are possible variations.[[2]](https://www.ucpress.edu/blog-posts/a-tomato-y-lentil-soup-for-cooler-days)\n\n**Background.** Lentils are among the earliest domesticated pulse crops, with archaeological remains in the Fertile Crescent dating back about 10,000 years. ICARDA cites lentil paste in ancient Egyptian tombs and a depiction of lentil soup from the era of Ramses II. The menu does not identify a specific national style, so these details describe a well-attested tradition rather than claiming this exact bowl is Egyptian.[[1]](https://repo.mel.cgiar.org/items/9df3d770-56ad-4214-b16a-f26d4052afb8)",
        "summary": "Lentil soup is a flexible, protein-rich pulse soup; the menu names the dish but gives no regional variant."
      }
